Restaurant Recommendation System
================================

This project is a restaurant recommender system using the Restaurant & Consumer
dataset. It includes two runnable versions:

1. A custom Vite web app built with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.
2. A Streamlit app built with Python for easier online sharing.

Recommendation Methods
----------------------

The app supports three selectable recommendation methods:

1. Item-Based Collaborative Filtering
   - Compares restaurants using shared user-rating patterns.
   - Uses cosine similarity between restaurant rating vectors.
   - Recommends restaurants similar to venues liked by the matched user.

2. Location-Aware Recommendation
   - Uses the selected dining area and restaurant latitude/longitude.
   - Calculates distance with the Haversine formula.
   - Prioritizes nearby restaurants while respecting filters.

3. Hybrid Recommendation
   - Combines multiple signals into one final score.
   - Uses rating quality, collaborative taste, preference fit, and convenience.
   - Default formula:

     Final score = 100 * (
       0.34 * quality +
       0.28 * item taste +
       0.24 * preference fit +
       0.14 * convenience
     )

Project Files
-------------

- `index.html` - main page for the Vite web app
- `app.js` - JavaScript recommender logic and UI behavior
- `styles.css` - custom web app styling
- `streamlit_app.py` - Streamlit version for sharing online
- `requirements.txt` - Python dependencies for Streamlit
- `dataset/` - restaurant, user, rating, cuisine, payment, parking, and hours CSV files
- `canva-restaurant-recommender.html` - presentation source imported into Canva

Run the Vite Web App
--------------------

Use this version when you want the full custom interface.

```bash
cd /Users/xkuan/Documents/Uni/1_2026/DSARS/project/restaurant
npm install
npm run dev
```

Then open the local URL printed by Vite, usually:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:5173/
```

If that port is busy, Vite may use another port such as `5174`.

Run the Streamlit App
---------------------

Use this version when you want to share the project more easily.

```bash
cd /Users/xkuan/Documents/Uni/1_2026/DSARS/project/restaurant
pip3 install -r requirements.txt
streamlit run streamlit_app.py
```

If `streamlit` is not found, use:

```bash
python3 -m streamlit run streamlit_app.py
```

Then open:

```text
http://localhost:8501
```

Share with Streamlit Community Cloud
------------------------------------

1. Create a GitHub repository.
2. Upload this `restaurant` project folder to the repository.
3. Make sure these files are included:
   - `streamlit_app.py`
   - `requirements.txt`
   - `dataset/`
4. Go to https://share.streamlit.io/
5. Sign in with GitHub.
6. Click **New app**.
7. Select your repository and branch.
8. Set the main file path to:

```text
streamlit_app.py
```

9. Click **Deploy**.

Streamlit will create a public URL that can be shared with others.

Notes
-----

- `node_modules/`, `dist/`, Python cache files, Streamlit secrets, and `.env`
  files are ignored by `.gitignore`.
- The Vite and Streamlit apps are not visually identical. The Vite version has
  the richer custom UI, while the Streamlit version is optimized for quick
  sharing and deployment.
- Both versions use the same dataset and the same three recommendation method
  concepts.

Original Dataset Description
============================

1. Title of Database: Restaurant & consumer data, recommender systems domain

2. Sources:
   (a) Creators: 
Rafael Ponce Medellín and Juan Gabriel González Serna
rafaponce@cenidet.edu.mx, gabriel@cenidet.edu.mx
Department of Computer Science.
National Center for Research and Technological Development CENIDET, México
   (b) Donors of database:
Blanca Vargas-Govea and Juan Gabriel González Serna
blanca.vargas@cenidet.edu.mx/blanca.vg@gmail.com, gabriel@cenidet.edu.mx
Department of Computer Science.
National Center for Research and Technological Development CENIDET, México

3. Past Usage:
    (a) Blanca Vargas-Govea, Juan Gabriel González-Serna, Rafael Ponce-Medellín. Effects of relevant contextual features in the performance of a restaurant recommender system. In RecSys’11: Workshop on Context Aware Recommender Systems (CARS-2011), Chicago, IL, USA, October 23, 2011.
    (b) The task was to generate a top-n list of restaurants according to the consumer preferences. Finding the significant features was another addressed problem.
4. Relevant Information.
Two approaches were tested: a collaborative filter technique and a contextual approach.
   (i) The collaborative filter technique used only one file i.e., rating_final.csv that comprises the user, item and rating attributes. 
   (ii) The contextual approach generated the recommendations using the remaining eight data files.


5. Files, instances and attributes
Number of Files: 9

